# tools/al/ales_reverb_gate_env_witness.rish -- the envelope gate: the reverb's GATED TAIL given a shaped edge, the time-following gated reverb (resonance-audio).
#
# Chapter: the Six-Chapter double-seat, Chapter C thread (Resonance - the creative suite) - waymark resonance-audio - rung resonance-audio.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-110731_fill-ales202-resonance-reverb-gate-env.md
# Stands on: resonance/reverb_preset.rye (resonance-audio -- the named-room wet, run over the span unchanged); resonance/gate_env.rye (resonance-audio -- the attack/release
# envelope gate and its guards, the gating applied to the wet span); resonance/reverb_gate.rye (resonance-audio -- the static gate this contains as its
# instantaneous limit); resonance/clock.rye (resonance-audio -- the sample clock, converting the span); resonance/timeline.rye (resonance-audio -- the Clip, its max_clip bounding
# the span and its sample_max the gate's rail).
#
# resonance/reverb_gate_env.rye gives the reverb's GATED TAIL a SHAPED EDGE -- a true time-following envelope gate that opens on the transient and closes
# over the decay, so the release holds the gate open through the tail rather than chopping it (a musical, breathing gated reverb beyond the hard
# eighties slam). Both resonance-audio and resonance-audio named this rung as their next horizon. It reverberates the span wet in place through resonance-audio's reverb_preset
# UNCHANGED, then gates the wet with resonance-audio's proven envelope-following gate: ratio_num == ratio_den is the identity, a larger ratio cuts the quiet
# tail harder, and a slower release holds the gate open longer. It invents no new audio arithmetic -- every gated sample is written by a proven stage --
# and it prechecks the gate's threshold, ratio, and both attack/release coefficients (the SAME guards resonance-audio raises) BEFORE the wet is written, so a
# bad setting leaves the clip DRY (atomic). The selftest proves: at ratio_num == ratio_den the span equals reverb_preset byte for byte; for a firm
# ratio reverb_gate_env equals reverb_preset then gate_follow byte for byte and the shaped-gated tail differs from the un-gated wet; with unit attack
# and unit release it equals resonance-audio's static reverb_gate byte for byte (the static gate is its zero-smoothing limit); the gated tail's total energy is
# strictly less than the wet's; a slower release carries at least as much tail energy as a faster one (the release shapes the edge, monotone); silence
# stays silence; and the faults forward atomic (a bad threshold BadThreshold, a bad ratio BadRatio, an illegal coefficient BadCoeff with the clip still
# dry, a span past the clip DurationTooLong, an out-of-range span BadRange). Purely local -- one clip, a validated clock; no network, no keys, no funds,
# no real device.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_reverb_gate_env_witness.rish

say "ales-reverb-gate-env: Language EN — resonance-audio/201 gave the reverb its STATIC gated tail (slam a downward gate across the quiet decay so only the loud early wash is heard — the eighties drum snare), and both named the SAME next horizon: a true time-following envelope gate that opens on the transient and closes over the decay, so the cut has a SHAPED edge rather than a hard, instantaneous one. This rung is that gate — and it invents no new primitive, since resonance-audio's gate_follow (the attack/release envelope gate over resonance-audio's proven time base) already stands, so the shaped-edge gated reverb is a clean composition of two proven stages."
say "ales-reverb-gate-env: Style Radiant — the identity edge is stated positively and proven exact: at ratio_num == ratio_den the span equals reverb_preset byte for byte (a unit-ratio follow-gate is the un-gated reverb); a larger ratio pushes the below-threshold tail harder toward silence, and a SLOWER release holds the gate open longer over the decay so the shaped tail carries at least as much energy as a faster release's — the release knob is the shaped edge the static gate cannot express, and it is monotone. The static gate (resonance-audio) lives on as this rung's zero-smoothing limit: unit attack and unit release make the envelope the bare magnitude, so reverb_gate_env then equals reverb_gate byte for byte."
say "ales-reverb-gate-env: Lens TAME — one clip of i16 PCM, a validated clock, and resonance-audio's fixed banks; NO new audio arithmetic — the gate is resonance-audio's proven envelope-following gate over the proven wet, its time base the one resonance-audio proved; ReverbGateEnvError = reverb_preset.ReverbPresetError || BadThreshold || BadRatio || BadCoeff, the gate's threshold, ratio, and both coefficients PRECHECKED before the wet is written (the same guards resonance-audio raises) so a bad gate leaves the clip dry (atomic), DurationTooLong / BadRange / BadThreshold / BadRatio / BadCoeff by name before any write; siloed to resonance/, no network, no keys, no funds, no device. A wet-tail envelope gate; the attack/release are fractions per sample index (a clock-timed face is a later rung, as reverb_ms followed the index-named reverb)."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
# THE BINARY LANDS IN A PEN, NEVER A SHARED PATH IN THE TREE (REDS %700). A fixed path is
# one name every run in this checkout writes: one run EXECUTES it while another's linker
# opens it, and the linker refuses with ETXTBSY -- so the guard answers differently on
# bytes nobody changed. `printf %s` trims the newline a bare capture would keep.
let pen = run ["sh" "-c" "printf %s $(mktemp -d)"]
assert pen.ok else "could not make a build pen: ${pen.err_brief}"
let bin = "${pen.out}/reverb_gate_env"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p resonance/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} sh tools/fixtures/r/rye_build.sh resonance/reverb_gate_env.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-reverb-gate-env: build failed — ${build.err_brief}"

let outfile = "resonance/bin/reverb_gate_env.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-reverb-gate-env: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-reverb-gate-env: ${shown.out_brief}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-reverb-gate-env' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-reverb-gate-env: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-reverb-gate-env line"

# This rung runs resonance-audio's reverb_preset over its public API for the wet -- re-prove the named-room preset still stands green beside it.
let preset = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} sh tools/fixtures/r/rye_build.sh resonance/reverb_preset.rye -femit-bin=resonance/bin/reverb_preset && resonance/bin/reverb_preset selftest 2>&1 | grep -q 'GREEN ales-reverb-preset' && echo yes"]
assert (trim preset.out) == "yes" else "ales-reverb-gate-env: the resonance-audio named-room preset regressed under its envelope gate"

# And re-prove resonance-audio's attack/release envelope gate still stands green -- the gating it applies to the wet.
let gateenv = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} sh tools/fixtures/r/rye_build.sh resonance/gate_env.rye -femit-bin=resonance/bin/gate_env && resonance/bin/gate_env selftest 2>&1 | grep -q 'GREEN ales-gate-env' && echo yes"]
assert (trim gateenv.out) == "yes" else "ales-reverb-gate-env: the resonance-audio envelope gate regressed under its reverb gate"

# And re-prove resonance-audio's static reverb gate still stands green -- the instantaneous limit this rung equals.
let staticgate = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} sh tools/fixtures/r/rye_build.sh resonance/reverb_gate.rye -femit-bin=resonance/bin/reverb_gate && resonance/bin/reverb_gate selftest 2>&1 | grep -q 'GREEN ales-reverb-gate' && echo yes"]
assert (trim staticgate.out) == "yes" else "ales-reverb-gate-env: the resonance-audio static reverb gate regressed beside its envelope gate"

say "ales-reverb-gate-env: GREEN — the envelope gate: the reverb's GATED TAIL given a shaped edge, the time-following gated reverb. Reverberate the span wet in place through resonance-audio's reverb_preset unchanged, then gate the wet with resonance-audio's proven envelope-following gate — ratio_num == ratio_den the identity, a larger ratio cutting the quiet tail harder, a slower release holding the gate open longer — no new audio arithmetic, every gated sample written by a proven stage. Proven: at ratio_num == ratio_den exactly reverb_preset byte for byte; for a firm ratio reverb_gate_env equals reverb_preset then gate_follow byte for byte and the shaped-gated tail differs from the un-gated wet; with unit attack and unit release it equals resonance-audio's static reverb_gate byte for byte; the gated tail's total energy is strictly less than the wet's; a slower release carries at least as much tail energy as a faster one; silence stays silence; a bad threshold refuses BadThreshold, a bad ratio BadRatio, an illegal coefficient BadCoeff with the clip still dry (prechecked before the wet is written), a span past the clip DurationTooLong, an out-of-range span BadRange, each leaving the clip untouched; the resonance-audio preset, the resonance-audio envelope gate, and the resonance-audio static gate stay GREEN beside it. A stereo envelope gate (the same follow-gate over both channels, one shared threshold, ratio, and attack/release, the image held) is the thin twin next."

# The pen is swept here rather than left for the next run to meet.
let pen_swept = run ["sh" "-c" "rm -rf ${pen.out}"]
assert pen_swept.ok else "the build pen outlived its witness: ${pen_swept.err_brief}"
